  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"108\" data-end=\"205\"><strong data-start=\"108\" data-end=\"138\">Urea levels in body fluids</strong> (such as pleural, peritoneal, or pericardial fluid) are tested to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"207\" data-end=\"467\">\n<li data-start=\"207\" data-end=\"270\">\n<p data-start=\"209\" data-end=\"270\">Differentiate <strong data-start=\"223\" data-end=\"245\">types of effusions</strong> (transudate vs. exudate)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"271\" data-end=\"369\">\n<p data-start=\"273\" data-end=\"369\">Diagnose <strong data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"306\">urinary tract injury</strong> or <strong data-start=\"310\" data-end=\"329\">urinary leakage</strong> (especially into the peritoneal cavity)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"370\" data-end=\"467\">\n<p data-start=\"372\" data-end=\"467\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"381\" data-end=\"398\">uroperitoneum</strong> or <strong data-start=\"402\" data-end=\"415\">urothorax</strong> (presence of urine in peritoneal or pleural cavity)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"469\" data-end=\"618\">Urea is a nitrogenous waste product normally present in blood and urine, and its <strong data-start=\"550\" data-end=\"587\">presence in excess within a fluid</strong> can indicate leakage of urine.</p>\n<p data-start=\"469\" data-end=\"618\">Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Insight</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bladder rupture / urinary tract injury</strong></td>\n<td>Leads to leakage of urine into peritoneal or pleural space; high urea in fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Uroperitoneum</strong></td>\n<td>Significantly elevated fluid urea, usually with creatinine; fluid-to-serum urea ratio &gt; 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Urinoma</strong></td>\n<td>Encapsulated urine collection due to trauma or obstruction; high urea content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Peritoneal dialysis (if recent)</strong></td>\n<td>May show elevated levels, especially shortly after treatment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"469\" data-end=\"618\">&nbsp;</p>",
